$ ~/go/bin/mygo version
mygo 0.7.1

$ mygo --help
mygo is the tool for MyGo desktop applications.

Usage:

	mygo <command> [arguments]

Commands:

	init [dir]           create a new project (Go + TypeScript frontend built with Vite)
	install-skills [dir] install or update the bundled MyGo agent skills
	vet [dir]           run Go vet and check native UI build lifetimes
	migrate-ui [dir]     preview or apply the checked UI value API migration
	generate             write the typed TypeScript client for bound Go services,
	                     and the accessors of the app's catalogs of messages
	i18n check [dir]     check the app's catalogs of messages
	dev                  run a development build with live reload
	build                build production apps (a .app and a .dmg on macOS)
	keygen               create the key pair that signs updates
	doctor               check that the development environment is ready
	version              print the MyGo version

Run "mygo <command> -h" for the flags of a command.

$ mygo init --help
Usage: mygo init [flags] <dir>

Creates a new MyGo project. The web template has a TypeScript frontend built
with Vite: Bun installs its dependencies, the mygo-cli package among them,
and runs its scripts, bun run dev and bun run build. The native template
shows a user interface MyGo draws itself, written in Go with package ui:
the module has the CLI as a tool, for go tool mygo dev and go tool mygo
build.

Flags:
  -module string
    	Go module path (default: directory name)
  -mygo string
    	path to a local checkout of MyGo to use via a replace directive
  -name string
    	application name (default: directory name)
  -template string
    	the project: web (a TypeScript frontend) or native (native UI in Go) (default "web")

$ mygo build --help
Usage: mygo build [flags] [dir]

Builds a production app for each platform. It runs buildCommand from
mygo.json, then compiles the app with the frontendDist files embedded, served
at mygo://localhost/. macOS gets a signed .app bundle and a
"<slug>-<version>-<arch>.dmg" disk image (slug in mygo.json, by default
the name in lower case), without the arch for darwin/universal, whose
window invites dragging the app to Applications; other platforms get an
executable. Linux also gets a Debian
package, and the app as <slug>-<version>-linux-<arch>.tar.gz with
install.sh, which installs it for the user in ~/.local, where it can update
itself. The contents of the
resources directory and the resources listed in mygo.json are copied into
the bundle's Contents/Resources, or next to the executable, and the programs
among them are signed with the app. Directories of resources named after a
platform, such as resources/darwin or resources/linux-amd64, only ship with
that platform's apps; darwin/universal combines darwin-arm64 and
darwin-amd64. MyGo needs no cgo, so any platform can be compiled from any
machine; signing and disk images need macOS. Windows also gets
"<slug>-setup-<version>-<arch>.exe", made with NSIS, which mygo build
downloads on Windows when it is not installed.

Set macos.signingIdentity in mygo.json (or -sign) to a Developer ID to ship
outside the Mac App Store, and macos.notarize to notarize the disk image.

With updates in mygo.json and the key of mygo keygen in
MYGO_UPDATER_PRIVATE_KEY, each platform also gets a signed update archive,
delta updates from the last versions published (updates.deltas), and
update-<platform>.json: publish them where updates point to.

The platform web (or js/wasm) builds an app of native UI for browsers into
<out>/web: index.html, which loads the app, Go's wasm_exec.js and the app's
WebAssembly, named by its content: a static site, to serve from anywhere.

Flags:
  -debug
    	keep development features such as the web inspector
  -o string
    	output directory (default: out from mygo.json or dist)
  -platform string
    	comma separated GOOS/GOARCH targets, e.g. darwin/universal,linux/amd64,windows/amd64, or web for browsers (default "darwin/arm64")
  -sign string
    	macOS signing identity (default: macos.signingIdentity, or "-" for ad hoc)
  -skip-build-command
    	do not run buildCommand
  -skip-dmg
    	do not create a disk image for macOS
  -skip-notarize
    	do not notarize even when macos.notarize is set
  -upload
    	upload the installers and updates to the GitHub release of this version (updates.github), as a draft, or to the bucket of updates.s3
